tter what happens with the  Special Counsel investigation the  Southern District of New York which you  were formerly the US Attorney of  currently has at least four  investigations open that we know about  campaign conspiracy and the Trump  Organization inauguration funding  Trump's super PAC funding and foreign  lobbying is that a lot on one sort of  person in their organization yeah I mean  they can handle it they can they can  come and walk at the same time  I mean look they do hundreds of  investigations and there are lots and  lots of talented people there and some  of the best people that I've ever met  and one of the things I that I'm pleased  about in the book is letting people know  about some of the folks who are not  famous who you don't see on television  whose names you might not know but are  the unsung heroes in keeping New York  safe and keeping the country safe I mean  one of the things you write about in the  book too is that investigations are hard  yeah they are yeah it's not just connect  the dots and follow the money  explain a little bit more about that  yeah you can apparently not to keep  going back to this you can have stuck  sextuplets in nine minutes if you can't  you can't do an investigation that  quickly you never know where an  investigation might lead you know one of  the things that I think it's important  to remember is when you begin  investigating excuse me  when you begin an investigation you have  to keep an open mind you can't have a  thesis in advance and sometimes you go  down the path and you think you're gonna  find criminality and it turns out not to  be so and sometimes you're gonna go down  the path you think it's a long shot and  you end up being able to prove a case so  you know I think in this age of Trump  when people jump to conclusions and  people chant lock her up and lock him up  it's good to take a step back and to  think about what justice means how it's  done how its warded what fair-mindedness  means what due process is those lessons  have been a little bit lost I think in  the last couple of years yeah you point  out we need to get back to that you also  talked about in the book I was  fascinated by this that prosecutors  often fascinated and repulsed by mob  activity we've got a really interesting  story in the news right now where the  apparent boss of the Gambino family was  assassinated outside his home and  everybody thought that it was a mob hit  so when you look at that case what are  you thinking and talk about being  fascinated and repulsed by mob activity  yeah you know we were talking before the  show  Gail about you have to be interested in  the subject matter and lots of probably  my favorite movies are mob movies I  think and I and I wanted to be a mob  prosecutor and I was when I was a lion  assistant I thought when that  assassination happened like everyone  else the first first thought is it must  be a mob war yes when the reputed head  of the Gambino crime family is shot to  death outside of his home but then as I  also point in the book repeatedly over  and over again things are not always  what they seem  and sometimes you think that a person  committed the crime and that's where you  know all the leads point and that's what  common sense tells you that get you in a  lot of trouble they can be miscarriages  of justice and it looks like it again we  should remain with an open mind that  maybe it was it had something to do with  a love interest and had nothing to do  with mob warfare you say ask a lot of  questions there's no such thing as a  dumb question  is that a question  I happen to think that's I was surprised  to hear you say it though no it's very  true I think it's true if you're if  you're a new person in a job like our  rookie assistant US Attorney's that I  used to say to them a surprise question  in a case is worse than a suppressed  piece of evidence because the only way  you can get true knowledge about subject  matter and make sure 